20:05:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right that the trope is widespread—it shows up in movies, sitcoms, watercooler jokes, and even in how some people describe their own workplaces. The phrase “hen house drama” exists precisely because so many people claim to have seen it. I’m not dismissing that you’ve heard this repeatedly, or that the idea feels universal. But the fact that a stereotype is common doesn’t make it a reliable guide for building a team, and I think it’s worth separating the popularity of a story from its accuracy. Let’s look honestly at why these stories are so “universally recognized”: - **We tell stories that fit the script.** When a mixed team has a meltdown, it’s rarely framed as “men being dramatic” or “mixed-gender catfights.” We use neutral language like “personality clash” or “politics.” But when an all-female team has the exact same kind of friction, it gets slotted into a pre-existing cultural narrative about women. The same behavior gets filed under a different, more dismissive label. This isn't a conscious conspiracy—it's cognitive bias. - **Confirmation bias and selective memory.** If you *expect* drama from all-women groups, you’ll notice and remember every tense meeting and every sidelong comment, and you’ll completely forget the all-female teams that hummed along productively. Those quiet success stories don’t become “office lore” because they aren’t juicy. Meanwhile, the one that imploded gets recounted for years. - **Double standards about directness.** We could just as easily spin a “universal truth” that all-male teams are constantly destroyed by testosterone-fueled power struggles, one-upmanship, and silent feuds that never get resolved because men are socialized to avoid emotional confrontation. In fact, some people *do* joke about that. But we don’t use it to avoid hiring men together. We assume they can be professional. The women deserve the same assumption. - **The self-fulfilling prophecy.** When a manager starts a project with the belief that the women are going to be dramatic, they unconsciously treat them with less respect, intervene in minor disagreements faster, or interpret normal assertiveness as proof of cattiness. That management behavior can then create the very tension that gets pointed to as proof. The manager never realizes they were part of the cause. Historically, there have been many “universally recognized truths” that were later shown to be prejudice dressed up as common sense. People once widely believed that having a woman pilot would lead to hysteria in a crisis, that integrated teams would fail because of “natural” racial friction, that remote work would collapse because employees can’t be trusted. These beliefs were held with the same “everybody knows” certainty, and they crumbled when structures, expectations, and leadership changed. So here’s my honest question to you, not to be argumentative but to be practical: **Do you want to be the kind of leader who designs teams around a widely repeated joke, or the kind who builds a team strong enough to prove the joke wrong?** If you go into this team expecting drama, you will see drama everywhere. If instead you set clear norms, foster direct communication, and treat your team members like the capable professionals they are, you’re giving them the best possible shot at being a quiet counterexample that no one makes jokes about—because they’re too busy getting the work done well. The trope is real as a cultural story. You don’t have to give it real estate in your project plan.
